<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chongzhen Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1611">1611</a>–<a href="/years/1644">1644</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Zhu Youjian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chongzhen Emperor (1611–1644) was the last emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>, reigning from 1627. Inheriting an empire beset by famine, peasant rebellions and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> invasions, he executed or dismissed many ministers and generals, including the commander Yuan Chonghuan. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-zicheng">Li Zicheng</a>'s rebels took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in April 1644, he hanged himself on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">Coal</a> Hill behind the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbidden-city">Forbidden City</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chongzhen-emperor.jpg" alt="Chongzhen Emperor" width="320" height="446" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chongzhen-Emperor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zhu Youjian was born on 6 February 1611, the fifth son of Zhu Changluo, the Taichang Emperor, and a low-ranking concubine, Lady Liu. When he was four his father had his mother executed, for reasons unknown, and she was buried in secret. He was raised by his father's other concubines in a lonely but quiet environment. All the Taichang Emperor's sons but two died before adulthood, and after his father's death in 1620 his elder brother Zhu Youjiao became the Tianqi Emperor and made him Prince of Xin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The Tianqi Emperor died in October 1627 without a surviving heir, and Zhu Youjian, about sixteen years old, took the throne as the Chongzhen Emperor, helped by Empress Zhang. He quickly eliminated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-zhongxian">Wei Zhongxian</a> and Madam Ke and others held to belong to the Wei-Ke conspiracy, and tried to stem the dynasty's decline, concentrating his reforms on the top ranks of the civil and military establishment. Corruption and an empty treasury left few capable ministers, and he was suspicious of his officials and feared factions. He executed dozens of field commanders, among them Yuan Chonghuan, who had directed the northern defence against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a>.</p><p>Drought and famine drove two great uprisings, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-xianzhong">Zhang Xianzhong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-zicheng">Li Zicheng</a>, both poor men from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>. Meanwhile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-taiji">Hong Taiji</a>, whose father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a> had united the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> tribes, declared himself emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> in 1636. From 1633 to 1644 the Great Plague of Jingshi devastated northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Li Zicheng took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> in 1641 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> the next year, and on the lunar New Year of 1644 he proclaimed himself king of the Shun dynasty. The emperor refused repeated proposals to move the court south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-zicheng">Li Zicheng</a>'s forces attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> on 24 April 1644. Rather than face capture, the emperor gathered the imperial household, killed Consort Yuan and Princess Zhaoren with his sword and severed the arm of Princess Changping. On 25 April he is said to have gone to the hill of Meishan, in present-day Jingshan Park, where he took his own life. Accounts differ on whether he left a note.</p><p>The Qing declared their aim to be avenging the emperor and defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-zicheng">Li Zicheng</a> at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanhai-pass">Shanhai Pass</a>. Because the emperor had not moved the court south, they took over a largely intact <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> bureaucracy. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-ming">Southern Ming</a> court was set up at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>, and its last emperor was executed in 1662. A modern account holds that the Chongzhen Emperor was not especially incompetent by the standards of the later Ming, but that his suspicion, impatience and stubbornness doomed a dynasty already far gone.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chongzhen Emperor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In April 1644 Li Zicheng's rebels captured Beijing and the <b>Chongzhen Emperor</b> took his own life; the Ming general Wu Sangui then allied with the Manchus, who defeated Li at Shanhai Pass and occupied Beijing, establishing the Qing dynasty there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>As Li Zicheng's rebels entered Beijing, the <b>Chongzhen Emperor</b> hanged himself on Coal Hill behind the Forbidden City.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-25">25 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>When Li Zicheng's Shun dynasty took Beijing in 1644 and the <b>Chongzhen Emperor</b> killed himself, officials in Nanjing enthroned the Prince of Fu as the Hongguang Emperor.</blockquote><a href="/state/southern-ming">Southern Ming</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Jingshan Park, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.9,116.41&amp;year=1632" aria-label="Beijing on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7418 736 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Chongzhen Emperor"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7868 1006h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7868 1006h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.9,116.41&amp;year=1632">What was here in 1632? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Poor in virtue and of contemptible personality, I have incurred the wrath of God on high. My Ministers have deceived me.&#8221;</p><footer>His last decree, written as Beijing fell, 1644 (in Giles, Gems of Chinese Literature) &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1923 &#183; <a href="https://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks08/0800161.txt" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg Australia</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I am ashamed to meet my ancestors; and therefore I myself take off my crown, and with my hair covering my face, await dismemberment at the hands of the rebels. Do not hurt a single one of my people!&#8221;</p><footer>His last decree, written as Beijing fell, 1644 (in Giles, Gems of Chinese Literature) &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1923 &#183; <a href="https://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks08/0800161.txt" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg Australia</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
